HiGHS in WebAssembly, vendored for Gaffer IQ's multi-week solver
(js/engine/solver/, docs/decision-engine-solver-plan.md).

Source: npm package "highs" 1.15.4 (https://github.com/lovasoa/highs-js, MIT),
which is HiGHS 1.15.1 (https://github.com/ERGO-Code/HiGHS, MIT) compiled to
WebAssembly. highs.mjs and highs.wasm are the package's build/highs.mjs and
build/highs.wasm, byte for byte. LICENSE is the package's, which names both
copyright holders (highs-js and HiGHS); HiGHS's own LICENSE.txt is the same
MIT text.

Nothing here is loaded by the page at start-up. The solver worker
(js/engine/solver/worker.js) is created on first use; it fetches highs.wasm
with its SHA-256 as the fetch's integrity, so a changed file is refused and
the planner keeps the single-move engine, and imports highs.mjs.

To update:
1. npm install highs@<version> in a scratch folder.
2. Copy build/highs.mjs, build/highs.wasm and LICENSE into a new folder,
   assets/vendor/highs/<version>/, and delete the old one.
3. Hash both files:
     openssl dgst -sha256 -binary highs.mjs | base64
     openssl dgst -sha256 -binary highs.wasm | base64
4. In js/config.js, DECISION_ENGINE.solver.vendor: set dir to the new folder
   and mjs and wasm to the new hashes.
5. In vercel.json, point the immutable Cache-Control header at the new folder.
   A version's folder is cached for a year, so a new version never reuses an
   old folder's name.
6. Update this README's versions, then run npm test: tests/engine/solver/
   vendor.test.js checks the files against the hashes, and the solved tests
   run on this build.
